\section{Is the triple dual of a vector space isomorphic to the dual in infinite dimension?}

\begin{question}
Is the triple dual of a vector space isomorphic to the dual in infinite dimension?
\end{question}

\begin{answer}
Excellent question. The short answer is: \textbf{No, in infinite dimensions, the triple dual $V^{***}$ is not necessarily isomorphic to the dual $V^*$.}

In fact, the relationship between a vector space and its duals becomes much more complex and nuanced in infinite dimensions. Let's break down why.

\subsection*{1. Recap: The Finite-Dimensional Case}

In finite dimensions, everything works very neatly. Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional vector space over a field $\FF$.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Dual Space ($V^*$)}: The space of all linear functionals $f: V \to \FF$. It's a standard theorem that $\dim(V^*) = \dim(V)$.
\item \textbf{Double Dual ($V^{**}$)}: The space of all linear functionals $\phi: V^* \to \FF$. There is a \textbf{natural map} $\iota: V \to V^{**}$ defined by $\iota(v)(f) = f(v)$ for all $f \in V^*$. This map is always injective. Since the dimensions are equal, $\iota$ is an \textbf{isomorphism}. We say $V$ is \textbf{reflexive} ($V \cong V^{**}$).
\item \textbf{Triple Dual ($V^{***}$)}: We can use the natural isomorphism from $V$ to $V^{**}$ to create an isomorphism between $V^*$ and $V^{***}$.
\begin{itemize}
\item Start with the natural map $\iota: V \to V^{**}$.
\item Take its dual map: $\iota^*: (V^{**})^* \to V^*$. Since $(V^{**})^* = V^{***}$, we have $\iota^*: V^{***} \to V^*$.
\item Because $\iota$ is an isomorphism, its dual map $\iota^*$ is also an isomorphism.
\item Therefore, $V^{***} \cong V^*$.
\end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

So, in finite dimensions, $V \cong V^{**}$ and $V^* \cong V^{***}$.

\subsection*{2. The Infinite-Dimensional Case}

In infinite dimensions, the natural map $\iota: V \to V^{**}$ is still injective, but it is \textbf{not surjective}. This means $V$ is a \textit{proper} subspace of $V^{**}$. We say $V$ is \textbf{not reflexive}.

This failure of reflexivity breaks the nice chain of isomorphisms.

\begin{itemize}
\item $V^{**}$ is "larger" than $V$.
\item $V^{***} = (V^{**})^*$ is the dual of this "larger" space.
\end{itemize}

It is highly unlikely that the dual of a "larger" space ($V^{**}$) would be isomorphic to the dual of the "smaller" original space ($V$).

\subsection*{A Concrete Example: $\ell^1$ and $\ell^\infty$}

Let's consider a classic example from functional analysis. Let $V = \ell^1$, the space of absolutely summable sequences.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Dual ($V^*$)}: It is a standard result that $(\ell^1)^* \cong \ell^\infty$, the space of bounded sequences.
\item \textbf{Double Dual ($V^{**}$)}: $(\ell^1)^{**} = (\ell^\infty)^*$. This is a \textbf{very large} space. It can be shown that $\ell^1$ sits inside $(\ell^\infty)^*$ via the natural map $\iota$, but $(\ell^\infty)^*$ also contains many other "strange" functionals that are not represented by any sequence in $\ell^1$. In fact, $(\ell^\infty)^*$ is isomorphic to the space of bounded finitely additive measures on the natural numbers, which is much larger than $\ell^1$.
\item \textbf{Triple Dual ($V^{***}$)}: $(\ell^1)^{***} = ((\ell^\infty)^*)^*$. This is the dual of a vast, complicated space.
\end{enumerate}

Now, the question is: Is $(\ell^1)^{***} \cong (\ell^1)^*$?
\begin{itemize}
\item $(\ell^1)^{***} = ((\ell^\infty)^*)^*$
\item $(\ell^1)^* \cong \ell^\infty$
\end{itemize}

So, we are asking if $((\ell^\infty)^*)^* \cong \ell^\infty$. This is equivalent to asking if $\ell^\infty$ is reflexive. But it is a well-known theorem that \textbf{$\ell^\infty$ is not reflexive}. Therefore, $(\ell^1)^{***} \not\cong (\ell^1)^*$.

In this case, $V^{***}$ is actually \textbf{much larger} than $V^*$.

\subsection*{Conclusion and Summary}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|}
\hline
\textbf{Dimension} & $\mathbf{V}$ vs $\mathbf{V^{**}}$ & $\mathbf{V^*}$ vs $\mathbf{V^{***}}$ \\
\hline
\textbf{Finite} & $V \cong V^{**}$ (reflexive) & $V^* \cong V^{***}$ \\
\hline
\textbf{Infinite} & $V \subsetneq V^{**}$ (not reflexive) & $V^* \not\cong V^{***}$ (generally $V^{***}$ is larger) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Comparison of dual spaces in finite vs infinite dimensions}
\end{table}

The key takeaway is that in infinite dimensions, the isomorphism $V^* \cong V^{***}$ \textbf{fails precisely because $V$ is not reflexive} ($V \not\cong V^{**}$). The triple dual is typically a much larger and more complex space than the single dual.
\end{answer} \section{Do the higher order duals ever become isomorphic?}

\begin{question}
Do the higher order duals ever become isomorphic?
\end{question}

\begin{answer}
Another excellent and deep question. The answer is a fascinating mix of "no," "sometimes," and "it depends on your set theory."

Let's denote the process of taking duals repeatedly:
\begin{itemize}
\item $V^{(0)} = V$
\item $V^{(1)} = V^*$
\item $V^{(2)} = V^{**}$
\item $V^{(3)} = V^{***}$
\item \ldots and so on.
\end{itemize}

We want to know if for some $n$, $V^{(n)} \cong V^{(n+k)}$ for all $k \ge 0$, creating a "cycle" of isomorphisms.

Here's the breakdown of what can happen.

\subsection*{1. The General, "Pathological" Case: No Stabilization}

For a "general" infinite-dimensional vector space $V$ (using the standard axioms of Zermelo-Fraenkel set theory with the Axiom of Choice, ZFC), the sequence of duals \textbf{grows forever in size}.

\begin{itemize}
\item Each dual space is strictly larger than the previous one in terms of algebraic dimension (a measure of the "number of basis vectors").
\item More precisely, if $\dim(V)$ is an infinite cardinal number $\kappa$, then $\dim(V^*) = |\FF|^{\kappa}$, which is strictly larger than $\kappa$. Applying the dual again gives an even larger dimension $|\FF|^{|\FF|^{\kappa}}$, and so on.
\item Since isomorphic spaces must have the same algebraic dimension, this means:
$V \not\cong V^* \not\cong V^{**} \not\cong V^{***} \not\cong \dots$
\end{itemize}

In this general case, the sequence of duals \textbf{never becomes isomorphic}. Each step gives you a fundamentally larger and more complex space.

\subsection*{2. The Special, "Nice" Case: Reflexive Spaces}

A Banach space $X$ is called \textbf{reflexive} if the natural map $\iota: X \to X^{**}$ is an isomorphism.

For reflexive spaces, the sequence of duals stabilizes \textit{immediately} at the second step and then enters a simple 2-cycle.

\begin{itemize}
\item $X^{(0)} = X$
\item $X^{(1)} = X^*$
\item $X^{(2)} = X^{**} \cong X$
\item $X^{(3)} = X^{***} \cong X^*$
\item $X^{(4)} = X^{(4)} \cong X^{**} \cong X$
\item \ldots
\end{itemize}

So, for a reflexive space $X$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $X^{(2k)} \cong X$ for all even orders.
\item $X^{(2k+1)} \cong X^*$ for all odd orders.
\end{itemize}

The sequence doesn't stabilize to a single space, but it enters a predictable and finite cycle of two isomorphism classes: $X$ and $X^*$.

\textbf{Examples of Reflexive Spaces:}
\begin{itemize}
\item All finite-dimensional spaces.
\item All Hilbert spaces (like $\ell^2$, the space of square-summable sequences).
\item The $L^p$ spaces for $1 < p < \infty$.
\end{itemize}

So, in this "nice" infinite-dimensional case, the higher duals \textit{do} become isomorphic to earlier ones, forming a 2-cycle.

\subsection*{3. The Bizarre, Set-Theoretic Case: The James' Space}

Now for the most surprising part, which directly answers your question about higher-order duals becoming isomorphic. There exists a famous and ingenious construction in Banach space theory called \textbf{James' Space}, denoted $J$.

James' Space $J$ has the following remarkable properties:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $J$ is \textbf{isomorphic} to its double dual: $J \cong J^{**}$.
\item However, the canonical embedding $\iota: J \to J^{**}$ is \textbf{not} surjective.
\item Consequently, $J$ is of \textbf{codimension 1} in $J^{**}$. This means $J^{**}$ is isomorphic to $J \oplus \RR$ (or $J \oplus \CC$ for complex spaces).
\end{enumerate}

Let's trace the duals for James' Space $J$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $J^{(0)} = J$
\item $J^{(1)} = J^*$
\item $J^{(2)} = J^{**} \cong J$
\item $J^{(3)} = J^{***} \cong (J^{**})^* \cong J^*$
\item $J^{(4)} = J^{(4)} \cong J^{**} \cong J$
\item \ldots
\end{itemize}

Just like in the reflexive case, the duals of James' Space enter a 2-cycle: $J, J^*, J, J^*, \dots$

This is a stunning example of an infinite-dimensional space where the higher duals \textit{do} become isomorphic, even though the space is not reflexive in the standard sense (because the \textit{natural} map isn't an isomorphism).

\subsection*{Summary}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Case} & \textbf{Description} & \textbf{Does it stabilize / become isomorphic?} \\
\hline
\textbf{General Infinite-Dim} & "Pathological" vector space under ZFC. & \textbf{No.} The duals grow forever in size: $V \not\cong V^* \not\cong V^{**} \not\cong \dots$ \\
\hline
\textbf{Reflexive Space} & "Nice" Banach space where $X \cong X^{**}$ naturally. & \textbf{Yes, in a 2-cycle.} $X^{(2k)} \cong X$ and $X^{(2k+1)} \cong X^*$. \\
\hline
\textbf{James' Space} & Ingenious construction where $J \cong J^{**}$ non-naturally. & \textbf{Yes, in a 2-cycle.} $J^{(2k)} \cong J$ and $J^{(2k+1)} \cong J^*$. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Behavior of higher-order duals in different cases}
\end{table}

\textbf{Conclusion:} The higher-order duals of an infinite-dimensional vector space do not generally become isomorphic. However, for specific, well-behaved spaces (like Hilbert spaces or the exotic James' space), they do become isomorphic, always falling into a simple 2-cycle where the even-order duals are isomorphic to the original space and the odd-order duals are isomorphic to the first dual. The "forever growing" scenario is the generic one, while the "stabilizing" scenario is a special property of remarkable spaces.
\end{answer} \section{I thought isomorphism meant injective and surjective so how can the embedding not be surjective?}

\begin{question}
I thought isomorphism meant injective and surjective so how can the embedding not be surjective?
\end{question}

\begin{answer}
You have hit on a crucial and subtle point that is the source of a great deal of depth and confusion in this area. Your understanding is correct, and the resolution lies in the distinction between two different concepts:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Isomorphism}
\item \textbf{The Natural Embedding (or Canonical Map)}
\end{enumerate}

Let's clarify this.

\subsection*{1. Isomorphism}

An isomorphism between two vector spaces $V$ and $W$ is a linear map $T: V \to W$ that is both \textbf{injective} and \textbf{surjective}.

\begin{itemize}
\item If such a map exists, we say $V$ and $W$ are \textbf{isomorphic} and write $V \cong W$.
\item The key point is that the existence of \textit{some} bijective linear map is enough to declare two spaces isomorphic. It doesn't have to be a "natural" or "obvious" one.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{2. The Natural Embedding}

For any vector space $V$, there is a specific, "canonical" way to map it into its double dual. This is the map $\iota: V \to V^{**}$ defined by:
\[
[\iota(v)](f) = f(v) \quad \text{for all } v \in V, f \in V^*.
\]
Let's break down its properties:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{It is always injective and linear.} You can prove that if $\iota(v) = 0$, then $f(v) = 0$ for every linear functional $f$, which forces $v = 0$.
\item \textbf{It is not always surjective.} Whether it's surjective or not depends entirely on the dimension of $V$.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{The Confusion Resolved: Two Different Maps}

The magic and the confusion arise because we are often talking about \textbf{two different maps} connecting $V$ and $V^{**}$.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Map A (The Natural Map):} $\iota: V \to V^{**}$. This map is \textit{defined for every V} and is always injective.
\item \textbf{Map B (An Isomorphism):} Some other linear bijection $\phi: V \to V^{**}$, which might be very "unnatural" and complicated to define.
\end{enumerate}

The relationship between these maps determines the property of a space.

\subsection*{Let's Apply This to the Three Cases}

\subsubsection*{Case 1: Finite-Dimensional Spaces}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{The Natural Map $\iota$:} Is both injective \textbf{and surjective}. Therefore, $\iota$ itself is an isomorphism.
\item \textbf{Conclusion:} The "obvious" canonical map is the isomorphism. Life is simple.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Case 2: Reflexive Banach Spaces (e.g., Hilbert spaces, $L^p$ for $1<p<\infty$)}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{The Natural Map $\iota$:} Is both injective \textbf{and surjective}. Just like in the finite-dimensional case, $\iota$ itself is an isomorphism.
\item \textbf{Conclusion:} Again, the canonical map is the isomorphism.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Case 3: James' Space $J$ (The Bizarre Case)}

This is the case that illustrates your question perfectly.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{The Natural Map $\iota$:} $\iota: J \to J^{**}$ is \textbf{injective but NOT surjective}. The image $\iota(J)$ is a \textbf{proper, closed subspace} of $J^{**}$. In fact, it has codimension 1, meaning $J^{**}$ is "one dimension larger" than $\iota(J)$.
\item \textbf{However...} Even though the \textit{natural map} $\iota$ is not an isomorphism, the spaces $J$ and $J^{**}$ are still \textit{isomorphic as Banach spaces}.
\item \textbf{How is this possible?} Because there exists a \textbf{different, non-canonical map} $\phi: J \to J^{**}$ that \textit{is} bijective. This map $\phi$ is not the "evaluation map" $\iota$. It's a cleverly constructed, unnatural isomorphism that essentially "re-packs" the elements of $J$ and $J^{**}$ to match them up one-to-one, even though the obvious attempt ($\iota$) fails.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Analogy:} Imagine a hotel with rooms numbered 1, 2, 3, \dots (this is $J$). The double dual $J^{**}$ is like a hotel with rooms numbered 0, 1, 2, 3, \dots The natural map $\iota$ tries to send room $n$ in the first hotel to room $n$ in the second hotel. This fails because room 0 in the second hotel has no counterpart in the first hotel. So $\iota$ is not surjective.

However, you can define a \textit{different} map $\phi$. You can tell the guest in room 1 to move to room 0, the guest in room 2 to move to room 1, and so on. This new map $\phi(n) = n-1$ is a bijection between the two sets of room numbers. The spaces (the sets of rooms) are the same "size", but the most obvious pairing rule doesn't work. You need a clever reshuffling.

\subsection*{Summary}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Space Type} & \textbf{Natural Map $\iota: V \to V^{**}$} & \textbf{Are $V$ and $V^{**}$ Isomorphic?} \\
\hline
\textbf{Finite-Dim} & \textbf{Is an isomorphism} (bijective) & \textbf{Yes, via $\iota$} \\
\hline
\textbf{Reflexive Banach} & \textbf{Is an isomorphism} (bijective) & \textbf{Yes, via $\iota$} \\
\hline
\textbf{James' Space $J$} & \textbf{Not an isomorphism} (injective but not surjective) & \textbf{Yes, but via a different, unnatural map $\phi$} \\
\hline
\textbf{General Inf-Dim (e.g., $\ell^1$)} & \textbf{Not an isomorphism} (injective but not surjective) & \textbf{No} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Relationship between natural maps and isomorphisms}
\end{table}

So, your intuition was spot on: for a map to be an isomorphism, it must be bijective. The resolution is that \textbf{isomorphism is a property of the spaces}, stating that \textit{some} bijective linear map exists between them. \textbf{Reflexivity} is a much stronger property, stating that the \textit{specific, natural map $\iota$} is that bijective linear map. James' Space shows that these two properties are not the same.
\end{answer}

