\section{What is the definition of a Schauder basis?}

\begin{question}
What is the definition of a Schauder basis?
\end{question}

\begin{answer}
A \textbf{Schauder basis} is a generalization of the concept of a basis from linear algebra to infinite-dimensional Banach spaces (and more generally, topological vector spaces). It allows us to represent every vector in the space as an \textbf{infinite linear combination} of basis elements.

\subsection*{Formal Definition}

Let $X$ be a Banach space. A sequence $\{e_n\}_{n=1}^\infty$ in $X$ is called a \textbf{Schauder basis} for $X$ if for every vector $x \in X$, there exists a \textbf{unique} sequence of scalars $\{\alpha_n\}_{n=1}^\infty$ such that:

\[
x = \sum_{n=1}^\infty \alpha_n e_n
\]

where the convergence is in the norm topology of $X$, i.e.:

\[
\lim_{N \to \infty} \left\| x - \sum_{n=1}^N \alpha_n e_n \right\| = 0
\]

\subsection*{Key Properties and Consequences}

\subsubsection*{1. Uniqueness of Representation}
The uniqueness requirement means that if $\sum_{n=1}^\infty \alpha_n e_n = \sum_{n=1}^\infty \beta_n e_n$, then $\alpha_n = \beta_n$ for all $n$.

\subsubsection*{2. Coordinate Functionals}
For a Schauder basis $\{e_n\}$, there exist unique continuous linear functionals $\{f_n\} \subset X^*$ called the \textbf{coordinate functionals} or \textbf{biorthogonal functionals} such that:

\[
f_n(e_m) = \delta_{nm} = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } n = m \\ 0 & \text{if } n \neq m \end{cases}
\]

and for every $x \in X$, we have:

\[
x = \sum_{n=1}^\infty f_n(x) e_n
\]

\subsubsection*{3. Basis Constant}
The \textbf{basis constant} is defined as:

\[
K = \sup_{N \geq 1} \left\| S_N \right\|
\]

where $S_N: X \to X$ are the \textbf{partial sum operators}:

\[
S_N(x) = \sum_{n=1}^N f_n(x) e_n
\]

A basis is called \textbf{monotone} if $K = 1$.

\subsection*{Examples}

\subsubsection*{1. Standard Basis in $\ell^p$ ($1 \leq p < \infty$)}
The sequence $\{e_n\}$ where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $e_1 = (1, 0, 0, 0, \dots)$
\item $e_2 = (0, 1, 0, 0, \dots)$
\item $e_3 = (0, 0, 1, 0, \dots)$
\item etc.
\end{itemize}
is a Schauder basis for $\ell^p$. For any $x = (x_1, x_2, x_3, \dots) \in $\ell^p$, we have:

\[
x = \sum_{n=1}^\infty x_n e_n
\]

\subsubsection*{2. Trigonometric System in $L^p[0,1]$}
The functions $\{1, \cos(2\pi nx), \sin(2\pi nx)\}_{n=1}^\infty$ form a Schauder basis for $L^p[0,1]$ when $1 < p < \infty$.

\subsubsection*{3. Haar System in $L^p[0,1]$}
The Haar system is an important example of a Schauder basis for $L^p[0,1]$ ($1 \leq p < \infty$).

\subsection*{Important Theorems}

\subsubsection*{Banach's Basis Theorem}
If $\{e_n\}$ is a Schauder basis for a Banach space $X$, then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item The coordinate functionals $\{f_n\}$ are continuous
\item The partial sum operators $S_N$ are continuous and $\sup_N \|S_N\| < \infty$
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection*{Approximation Property}
A Banach space with a Schauder basis has the \textbf{approximation property}, meaning that compact operators can be approximated by finite-rank operators.

\subsection*{Comparison with Other Basis Concepts}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Basis Type} & \textbf{Space} & \textbf{Representation} & \textbf{Key Feature} \\
\hline
\textbf{Hamel Basis} & Any vector space & Finite linear combinations & Pure algebra, no topology \\
\hline
\textbf{Schauder Basis} & Banach spaces & Infinite series & Topological convergence \\
\hline
\textbf{Orthonormal Basis} & Hilbert spaces & Infinite series + orthogonality & Geometry + topology \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Comparison of basis concepts}
\end{table}

\subsection*{Limitations and Important Facts}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Not every Banach space has a Schauder basis} - Enflo (1973) constructed a separable Banach space without the approximation property, hence without a Schauder basis.

\item \textbf{A Schauder basis is not a Hamel basis} - In infinite dimensions, a Schauder basis is always linearly independent but doesn't span the space algebraically (only topologically).

\item \textbf{Unconditional vs Conditional} - A Schauder basis is \textbf{unconditional} if every convergent rearrangement of the series represents the same vector. Otherwise, it's \textbf{conditional}.

\item \textbf{Separability} - If a Banach space has a Schauder basis, it must be \textbf{separable} (since finite rational linear combinations of basis elements form a countable dense set).
\end{enumerate}

The concept of a Schauder basis is fundamental in functional analysis because it provides a way to work with infinite-dimensional spaces using sequence representations, much like we do in finite dimensions.
\end{answer}

File 9: 07_question_different_bases_different_injections.tex
