\section{Are the injections defined by biorthogonality different for different Schauder bases?}

\begin{question}
Are the injections defined by biorthogonality different for different Schauder bases?
\end{question}

\begin{answer}
\textbf{Yes, absolutely!} The injections defined by biorthogonality can be (and usually are) \textbf{completely different} for different Schauder bases of the same Banach space.

Let me explain why this happens and give some concrete examples.

\subsection*{1. The General Construction}

For a Schauder basis $\{e_n\}$ with biorthogonal functionals $\{f_n\} \subset X^*$, we can define an injection $\Phi: X \to X^*$ by:

\[
\Phi\left(\sum_{n=1}^\infty a_n e_n\right) = \sum_{n=1}^\infty a_n f_n
\]

This map sends each basis vector $e_n$ to its corresponding dual functional $f_n$.

\subsection*{2. Why Different Bases Give Different Injections}

\subsubsection*{A. Different Coordinate Systems}
Different Schauder bases represent vectors using different "coordinate systems":

\begin{itemize}
\item If $x = \sum a_n e_n$ in basis $\{e_n\}$
\item And $x = \sum b_n g_n$ in basis $\{g_n\}$
\item Then typically $a_n \neq b_n$
\end{itemize}

Since $\Phi_{\{e_n\}}(x) = \sum a_n f_n$ and $\Phi_{\{g_n\}}(x) = \sum b_n h_n$, these are different functionals acting on $X$.

\subsubsection*{B. Different Ranges}
The image of each injection is the closed linear span of the corresponding biorthogonal functionals:

\begin{itemize}
\item $\text{range}(\Phi_{\{e_n\}}) = \overline{\text{span}}\{f_n\}$
\item $\text{range}(\Phi_{\{g_n\}}) = \overline{\text{span}}\{h_n\}$
\end{itemize}

These can be different subspaces of $X^*$.

\subsection*{3. Concrete Example in $\ell^p$}

Let's consider $X = \ell^p$ with $1 < p < \infty$.

\subsubsection*{Basis 1: Standard Basis}
Let $\{e_n\}$ be the standard basis:
\begin{itemize}
\item $e_1 = (1,0,0,\dots)$
\item $e_2 = (0,1,0,\dots)$, etc.
\end{itemize}

The biorthogonal functionals are $f_n(x) = x_n$ (the $n$-th coordinate).

The injection $\Phi_{\text{std}}$ sends:
\[
\Phi_{\text{std}}\left(\sum a_n e_n\right) = \sum a_n f_n
\]
This is essentially the \textbf{identity map} from $\ell^p$ to $\ell^q$ (where $\frac{1}{p} + \frac{1}{q} = 1$).

\subsubsection*{Basis 2: "Shifted" Basis}
Define $g_1 = e_1$, and for $n \geq 2$:
\[
g_n = e_1 + e_n
\]

This is also a Schauder basis for $\ell^p$. The biorthogonal functionals are:
\begin{itemize}
\item $h_1(x) = x_1 - \sum_{n=2}^\infty x_n$
\item $h_n(x) = x_n$ for $n \geq 2$
\end{itemize}

Now the injection $\Phi_{\text{shift}}$ sends:
\[
\Phi_{\text{shift}}\left(\sum a_n g_n\right) = \sum a_n h_n
\]

\textbf{These are clearly different maps!} For example, take $x = (1,0,0,\dots) = e_1$:

\begin{itemize}
\item In standard basis: $x = 1\cdot e_1 + 0\cdot e_2 + \cdots$ \\
So $\Phi_{\text{std}}(x) = f_1$ (the functional that picks out the first coordinate)

\item In shifted basis: $x = 1\cdot g_1 + 0\cdot g_2 + \cdots$ \\
So $\Phi_{\text{shift}}(x) = h_1$ (the functional $h_1(x) = x_1 - \sum_{n=2}^\infty x_n$)
\end{itemize}

These are different linear functionals on $\ell^p$.

\subsection*{4. Even More Dramatic Example}

Consider a space with both an \textbf{unconditional basis} and a \textbf{conditional basis} (like certain function spaces).

\subsubsection*{Unconditional Basis:}
The injection $\Phi_{\text{uncond}}$ will have the property that the series $\sum a_n f_n$ converges \textbf{unconditionally} for every $x$.

\subsubsection*{Conditional Basis:}
The injection $\Phi_{\text{cond}}$ might only have conditional convergence for some vectors.

This means the topological properties of the two injections can be fundamentally different!

\subsection*{5. Properties That Can Differ}

For different Schauder bases $\{e_n\}$ and $\{g_n\}$ of the same space $X$, the injections $\Phi_{\{e_n\}}$ and $\Phi_{\{g_n\}}$ can have:

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Property} & \textbf{Can differ?} & \textbf{Example} \\
\hline
\textbf{Range} & Yes & $\overline{\text{span}}\{f_n\} \neq \overline{\text{span}}\{h_n\}$ \\
\hline
\textbf{Norm} & Yes & $\|\Phi_{\{e_n\}}\| \neq \|\Phi_{\{g_n\}}\|$ \\
\hline
\textbf{Continuity} & Yes & One might be bounded, the other unbounded* \\
\hline
\textbf{Injectivity} & Usually both injective & But kernels could differ \\
\hline
\textbf{Type of convergence} & Yes & Unconditional vs conditional \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Properties that can differ between biorthogonal injections}
\end{table}

*Note: For Schauder bases, the coordinate functionals are always bounded, so the injections are typically bounded operators. However, their operator norms can differ.

\subsection*{6. Special Case: Equivalent Bases}

Two Schauder bases $\{e_n\}$ and $\{g_n\}$ are called \textbf{equivalent} if there exists an isomorphism $T: X \to X$ such that $Te_n = g_n$ for all $n$.

\textbf{Theorem:} If two bases are equivalent, then the corresponding biorthogonal injections are "similar" in the sense that:
\[
\Phi_{\{g_n\}} = \Phi_{\{e_n\}} \circ T^{-1}
\]

So equivalent bases give injections that are just conjugates of each other by an automorphism of $X$.

\subsection*{Summary}

\textbf{Yes, different Schauder bases generally give different biorthogonal injections $X \to X^*$.} The injection depends crucially on:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{How the basis represents vectors} (the coordinate system)
\item \textbf{The biorthogonal functionals} (which solve a specific interpolation problem)
\item \textbf{The topological properties} of the basis (unconditional, conditional, etc.)
\end{enumerate}

This reflects the deep fact that in infinite dimensions, the relationship between a space and its dual is not canonical - it depends on choices we make (like which basis to use).
\end{answer}

