\section{Is there a name for spaces that have biorthogonal systems?}

\begin{question}
Is there a name for spaces that have biorthogonal systems?
\end{question}

\begin{answer}
\textbf{Yes!} There are several important names and classifications for spaces with biorthogonal systems. The terminology depends on exactly what kind of biorthogonal system you have.

\subsection*{1. The General Case: Spaces with Biorthogonal Systems}

A Banach space $X$ is said to \textbf{admit a biorthogonal system} if there exist $\{x_i\}_{i\in I} \subset X$ and $\{f_i\}_{i\in I} \subset X^*$ with $f_i(x_j) = \delta_{ij}$.

\textbf{This property is actually very common} - most "reasonable" Banach spaces have biorthogonal systems.

\subsection*{2. Important Special Cases with Specific Names}

\subsubsection*{A. Spaces with Markushevich Basis (M-basis)}
This is probably the most important and well-studied case.

An \textbf{M-basis} is a biorthogonal system $\{x_i, f_i\}$ where:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\{x_i\}$ is \textbf{fundamental}: $\overline{\text{span}}\{x_i\} = X$
\item $\{f_i\}$ is \textbf{total}: if $f_i(x) = 0$ for all $i$, then $x = 0$
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Properties:}
\begin{itemize}
\item Gives a continuous injection $X \to X^*$
\item Every separable Banach space has an M-basis! (Markushevich, 1943)
\item But the biorthogonal functionals might not be uniformly bounded
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{B. Spaces with Schauder Basis}
As we've discussed - this is the "gold standard" where everything works nicely.

\subsubsection*{C. Spaces with Unconditional Basis}
Even stronger - the basis representations converge unconditionally.

\subsubsection*{D. Spaces with Bounded M-basis}
An M-basis where $\sup_i \|x_i\|\|f_i\| < \infty$.
This ensures the associated projection operators are uniformly bounded.

\subsection*{3. Hierarchy of Properties}

Here's how these concepts relate:

\[
\text{Schauder Basis} \Rightarrow \text{Unconditional Basis (sometimes)}
\]
\[
\Rightarrow \text{Bounded M-basis} \Rightarrow \text{Markushevich Basis} \Rightarrow \text{Existence of Biorthogonal System}
\]

\textbf{Key facts:}
\begin{itemize}
\item Every separable Banach space has an M-basis
\item But not every separable Banach space has a Schauder basis (Enflo)
\item The step from M-basis to Schauder basis is huge
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{4. Special Names for Dual Relationships}

\subsubsection*{A. Quasi-reflexive Spaces}
Spaces where $\dim(X^{**}/X) < \infty$. These often have very nice biorthogonal structure.

\subsubsection*{B. Spaces with the Density Property}
$X$ has the density property if there exists an injection $X \to X^*$ with dense range. This is equivalent to having a total biorthogonal system.

\subsubsection*{C. Locally Convex Spaces with Equicontinuous Basis}
In the broader context of locally convex spaces, this is the generalization of Schauder bases.

\subsection*{5. Negative Results and Limitations}

\subsubsection*{A. The Johnson-Lindenstrauss Spaces}
There exist Banach spaces (JL spaces) that are \textbf{hereditarily indecomposable} - they have no nontrivial biorthogonal systems with special properties.

\subsubsection*{B. Spaces Without M-bases}
While every separable Banach space has an M-basis, there exist \textbf{non-separable} Banach spaces without M-bases.

\subsection*{6. Applications to Specific Spaces}

\subsubsection*{Classical Sequence Spaces}
\begin{itemize}
\item $\ell^p$ ($1 \leq p < \infty$): Have unconditional bases
\item $c_0$: Has Schauder basis
\item $\ell^\infty$: Has no Schauder basis (not separable) but has biorthogonal systems
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Function Spaces}  
\begin{itemize}
\item $L^p[0,1]$ ($1 < p < \infty$): Have unconditional bases
\item $C[0,1]$: Has Schauder basis (Faber-Schauder)
\item $L^1[0,1]$: Has no unconditional basis
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{7. Modern Research Directions}

Current research focuses on:

\subsubsection*{A. Biorthogonal Systems in Non-separable Spaces}
Understanding which non-separable spaces have "nice" biorthogonal systems.

\subsubsection*{B. Applications to Operator Theory}
Using biorthogonal systems to study the structure of operators.

\subsubsection*{C. Connections to Set Theory}
Some questions about existence of biorthogonal systems are independent of ZFC.

\subsection*{Summary}

\textbf{The main terminology is:}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{"Spaces with biorthogonal systems"} (most general)
\item \textbf{"Spaces with Markushevich bases"} (very common for separable spaces)  
\item \textbf{"Spaces with Schauder bases"} (strong and useful)
\item \textbf{"Spaces with unconditional bases"} (even stronger)
\end{itemize}

For a separable Banach space, having an M-basis is essentially the \textbf{weakest reasonable biorthogonal structure} - it's enough to get a continuous injection into the dual, but doesn't guarantee the nice computational properties of a Schauder basis.

This hierarchy explains why distribution theory can work with both $\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ (beautiful Schauder basis) and $\mathcal{D}(\Omega)$ (only general biorthogonal systems) - both have enough structure to define distributions as dual elements, but the quality of that structure differs dramatically!
\end{answer}

