\section{Can one define injections from a vector space to its dual by biorthogonality?}

\begin{question}
Can one define injections from a vector space to its dual by biorthogonality?
\end{question}

\begin{answer}
Excellent question! The short answer is \textbf{yes, but with very strong caveats}. The concept of biorthogonality is a powerful tool for defining injections from a vector space to its dual, but it works well only in specific, well-structured situations.

Let's explore this in detail.

\subsection*{1. The Finite-Dimensional Case: Perfect Biorthogonality}

In finite dimensions, biorthogonality gives us a perfect way to create isomorphisms (not just injections) between $V$ and $V^*$.

Let $V$ be finite-dimensional with basis $\{e_1, \dots, e_n\}$. We can define the \textbf{dual basis} $\{f_1, \dots, f_n\} \subset V^*$ by:
\[
f_i(e_j) = \delta_{ij} = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } i = j \\ 0 & \text{if } i \neq j \end{cases}
\]

The biorthogonality map $B: V \to V^*$ is defined by linearly extending:
\[
B(e_i) = f_i
\]
This map $B$ is an \textbf{isomorphism} (bijective). Every vector $v = \sum v_i e_i$ maps to the functional $B(v) = \sum v_i f_i$.

\textbf{Key point:} In finite dimensions, biorthogonal systems always exist and give us isomorphisms.

\subsection*{2. The Infinite-Dimensional Challenge}

In infinite dimensions, things become much more difficult. The main issues are:

\subsubsection*{A. Hamel Bases and Their Limitations}

Every vector space has a Hamel basis (by the Axiom of Choice), but:
\begin{itemize}
\item Hamel bases are uncountable for infinite-dimensional spaces
\item The dual elements defined via biorthogonality won't necessarily be continuous/bounded
\item The resulting map to the algebraic dual might not land in the topological dual we care about
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{B. The Minimal Requirement: A Biorthogonal System}

A \textbf{biorthogonal system} is a pair $\{x_i\}_{i\in I} \subset V$, $\{f_i\}_{i\in I} \subset V^*$ such that:
\[
f_i(x_j) = \delta_{ij}
\]

\textbf{Theorem:} A biorthogonal system exists if and only if $\dim(V) \leq \dim(V^*)$.

This gives us an \textbf{injection} $V \to V^*$ defined by $x_i \mapsto f_i$ (extended linearly).

\subsection*{3. When Biorthogonality Gives Nice Injections}

\subsubsection*{Case 1: Hilbert Spaces (The Nicest Case)}

In a Hilbert space $H$, the Riesz Representation Theorem gives us a \textbf{conjugate-linear isomorphism} $R: H \to H^*$ defined by:
\[
R(x)(y) = \langle y, x \rangle
\]

If we have an orthonormal basis $\{e_i\}_{i\in I}$, then biorthogonality appears naturally:
\begin{itemize}
\item The dual elements are $f_i = R(e_i)$
\item $f_i(e_j) = \langle e_j, e_i \rangle = \delta_{ij}$
\end{itemize}

Here, biorthogonality gives us a very nice (conjugate-linear) isomorphism.

\subsubsection*{Case 2: Banach Spaces with Schauder Bases}

Let $V$ be a Banach space with a Schauder basis $\{e_i\}_{i=1}^\infty$. We can define the \textbf{biorthogonal functionals} $\{f_i\}_{i=1}^\infty \subset V^*$ by:
\[
f_i(e_j) = \delta_{ij}
\]

These biorthogonal functionals allow us to define an injection $\Phi: V \to V^*$ by:
\[
\Phi\left(\sum_{i=1}^\infty a_i e_i\right) = \sum_{i=1}^\infty a_i f_i
\]

\textbf{However, there are serious limitations:}
\begin{enumerate}
\item This map is \textbf{not necessarily surjective} - its image is the closed linear span of $\{f_i\}$
\item The map might \textbf{not be bounded/continuous}
\item Even if bounded, it might not have closed range
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection*{Case 3: Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Spaces (RKHS)}

In an RKHS $\mathcal{H}$ of functions on a set $X$, biorthogonality appears in a beautiful way through the \textbf{reproducing property}:
\[
f(x) = \langle f, K_x \rangle
\]
where $K_x \in \mathcal{H}$ is the reproducing kernel at $x$.

The evaluation functionals $\delta_x: f \mapsto f(x)$ correspond to $K_x$ via the Riesz map:
\[
\delta_x = R(K_x)
\]
This gives us a very natural biorthogonal-like structure.

\subsection*{4. Obstructions and Limitations}

\subsubsection*{A. James' Space Strikes Again}

Recall James' space $J$ has the property that $J \cong J^{**}$ but the natural embedding isn't surjective. Even with biorthogonal systems, we cannot get a "nice" isomorphism.

\subsubsection*{B. The Issue of Completeness}

A biorthogonal system $\{x_i, f_i\}$ is called:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Complete} if $\{f_i\}$ separates points of $V$ (i.e., if $f_i(x) = 0$ for all $i$, then $x = 0$)
\item \textbf{Total} if $\{x_i\}$ separates points of $V^*$
\end{itemize}

Even with a complete biorthogonal system, the associated map $V \to V^*$ might not be surjective.

\subsubsection*{C. Counterexamples Exist}

There are Banach spaces with Schauder bases where no biorthogonal system can generate an isomorphism between $V$ and $V^*$. The spaces $\ell^1$ and $\ell^\infty$ provide such examples:
\begin{itemize}
\item $(\ell^1)^* \cong \ell^\infty$
\item But $\ell^1 \not\cong \ell^\infty$, so no biorthogonal system can give an isomorphism
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Summary}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Situation} & \textbf{Can biorthogonality define injection $V \to V^*$?} & \textbf{Quality of the injection} \\
\hline
\textbf{Finite dimensions} & Yes, always & Isomorphism \\
\hline
\textbf{Hilbert spaces} & Yes & Conjugate-linear isomorphism \\
\hline
\textbf{Banach spaces with basis} & Yes, often & Injection, rarely surjective \\
\hline
\textbf{General Banach spaces} & Maybe (depends on existence of biorthogonal system) & Usually poor properties \\
\hline
\textbf{Without AC} & Unknown/maybe not & - \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Biorthogonal injections in different contexts}
\end{table}

\textbf{Conclusion:} Biorthogonality can indeed define injections from a vector space to its dual, but these injections are:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Isomorphisms} only in very special cases (finite dimensions, Hilbert spaces)
\item \textbf{Proper injections} in most infinite-dimensional Banach spaces
\item \textbf{Nonexistent or pathological} in some spaces
\end{itemize}

The strength of the injection depends crucially on the structure of the space and the completeness properties of the biorthogonal system.
\end{answer}

