\section{Is there a name for Banach spaces that have Schauder bases?}

\begin{question}
Is there a name for Banach spaces that have Schauder bases?
\end{question}

\begin{answer}
Yes, there are several important names and concepts related to Banach spaces with Schauder bases:

\subsection*{1. Spaces with the "Basis Property"}

A Banach space is said to have the \textbf{basis property} if it admits a Schauder basis. However, this terminology is somewhat informal.

\subsection*{2. More Precisely: Spaces with the "Approximation Property"}

There's a fundamental hierarchy:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Schauder basis} $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{Approximation property}
\item But \textbf{Approximation property} $\nRightarrow$ \textbf{Schauder basis}
\end{itemize}

The \textbf{approximation property} means that compact operators can be approximated by finite-rank operators. Every space with a Schauder basis has the approximation property, but the converse is false (as shown by Enflo's famous counterexample).

\subsection*{3. Important Subclasses}

\subsubsection*{A. Spaces with Unconditional Bases}
These are Banach spaces that admit an \textbf{unconditional Schauder basis} - where every convergent rearrangement of the basis representation converges to the same vector.

Examples include:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\ell^p$ spaces ($1 \leq p < \infty$)
\item $c_0$
\item $L^p[0,1]$ for $1 < p < \infty$
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{B. Spaces with Boundedly Complete Bases}
A basis $\{e_n\}$ is \textbf{boundedly complete} if whenever $\sup_N \|\sum_{n=1}^N a_n e_n\| < \infty$, then $\sum_{n=1}^\infty a_n e_n$ converges.

\subsubsection*{C. Spaces with Shrinking Bases}
A basis is \textbf{shrinking} if the biorthogonal functionals $\{f_n\}$ form a basis for $X^*$.

\subsection*{4. Major Open Problems (Now Mostly Resolved)}

\subsubsection*{The Basis Problem}
For decades, the \textbf{basis problem} asked: "Does every separable Banach space have a Schauder basis?"

This was \textbf{settled negatively by Per Enflo in 1973}, who constructed a separable Banach space without the approximation property (and hence without a Schauder basis).

\subsubsection*{The Approximation Property Problem}
"Does every Banach space have the approximation property?" This was also settled negatively by Enflo.

\subsection*{5. Positive Results: Important Classes That Always Have Bases}

Many classical spaces are known to have Schauder bases:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{All separable Hilbert spaces} (orthonormal bases)
\item \textbf{$\ell^p$ spaces} ($1 \leq p < \infty$)
\item \textbf{$c_0$} (the space of sequences converging to 0)
\item \textbf{$L^p[0,1]$ for $1 < p < \infty$}
\item \textbf{$C[0,1]$} (the space of continuous functions) - has the Faber-Schauder basis
\item \textbf{Sobolev spaces} $W^{k,p}$ on nice domains
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{6. Modern Terminology}

In contemporary literature, one typically says:

\begin{itemize}
\item "\textbf{X has a Schauder basis}" (specific property)
\item "\textbf{X has the approximation property}" (weaker property)
\item "\textbf{X has an unconditional basis}" (stronger property)
\end{itemize}

There's no single elegant name like "based spaces" because:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Not all "nice" separable spaces have bases
\item The property is not preserved under standard operations (subspaces, quotients)
\item It's not a categorical or isomorphic invariant
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Summary}

While there's no universally accepted single name like "based Banach spaces," the most precise descriptions are:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{"Spaces with a Schauder basis"}
\item \textbf{"Spaces with the basis property"} (less common)
\item More specifically: \textbf{"Spaces with an unconditional basis"} when applicable
\end{itemize}

The study of which Banach spaces have Schauder bases, and what kind of bases they have, remains an active and important area of research in functional analysis, with deep connections to approximation theory, harmonic analysis, and operator theory.
\end{answer}

