\section{In point 3 do all separable dense subsets have Schauder bases?}

\begin{question}
In point 3 do all separable dense subsets have Schauder bases?
\end{question}

\begin{answer}
\textbf{Excellent question!} The answer is a resounding \textbf{NO} - this is a crucial subtlety that often catches people by surprise.

\subsection*{1. The Short Answer}

\textbf{Separability (existence of a countable dense subset) does NOT imply the existence of a Schauder basis.}

These are \textbf{independent properties}:
\begin{itemize}
\item Separability = "small" in the topological sense
\item Schauder basis = "good coordinate system" in the linear sense
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{2. Counterexamples Exist!}

\subsubsection*{Per Enflo's Famous Result (1973)}
Enflo constructed a \textbf{separable Banach space that lacks the approximation property} (and hence has no Schauder basis).

This was a major breakthrough that settled a long-standing open problem.

\subsubsection*{Concrete Examples}
\begin{itemize}
\item There exist separable Banach spaces with no Schauder basis
\item Even some \textbf{separable reflexive Banach spaces} lack Schauder bases
\item The space of compact operators on $\ell^2$ is separable but may not have a basis
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{3. Why This Separation Occurs}

\subsubsection*{Separability is About Topology}
A space is separable if there exists a countable set $D$ such that every open set contains an element of $D$.

\textbf{This is purely topological} - it doesn't care about linear structure.

\subsubsection*{Schauder Basis is About Linear Structure}  
A Schauder basis requires:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Linear independence
\item Unique representations as convergent series
\item Continuous coordinate functionals
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{This is deeply algebraic-topological} - it requires the topology and linear structure to interact nicely.

\subsection*{4. The Hierarchy of Properties}

Let's organize what we know:

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Property} & \textbf{Strength} & \textbf{Relationship} \\
\hline
\textbf{Finite-dimensional} & Strongest & $\Rightarrow$ Has basis, $\Rightarrow$ separable \\
\hline
\textbf{Schauder basis} & Strong & $\Rightarrow$ Separable, $\Rightarrow$ Approximation property \\
\hline
\textbf{Separable} & Weak & $\nRightarrow$ Schauder basis \\
\hline
\textbf{Banach space} & Weakest & $\nRightarrow$ Separable, $\nRightarrow$ Basis \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Hierarchy of properties in Banach spaces}
\end{table}

\textbf{Key:} Schauder basis $\Rightarrow$ Separable, but \textbf{Separable $\nRightarrow$ Schauder basis}

\subsection*{5. Back to Distribution Theory}

This explains the different behavior of our distribution spaces:

\subsubsection*{$\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ - The "Nice" Case}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Separable} (polynomials with rational coefficients)
\item \textbf{Has Schauder basis} (Hermite functions)
\item Everything works beautifully
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{$\mathcal{D}(\Omega)$ - The "Pathological" Case}  
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Separable} (countable dense sets exist)
\item \textbf{No Schauder basis} (too complicated topologically)
\item We must rely on separability alone
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{6. Practical Implications}

\subsubsection*{When You Have a Basis ($\mathcal{S}$)}
You can do \textbf{explicit computations}:
\begin{itemize}
\item Fourier expansions
\item Hermite expansions  
\item Galerkin methods
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{When You Only Have Separability ($\mathcal{D}$)}
You use \textbf{approximation arguments}:
\begin{itemize}
\item "For any $\epsilon > 0$, there exists $\phi$ in our countable dense set..."
\item Weak continuity arguments
\item Localization techniques
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{7. The Silver Lining: Separability Still Helps}

Even without a basis, separability gives us:

\subsubsection*{Sequential Arguments}
In separable spaces, we can often work with sequences rather than nets/filters.

\subsubsection*{Countable Determination}
A continuous linear functional is determined by its values on a countable dense set.

\subsubsection*{Approximation Schemes}
We can construct finite-dimensional approximations using the dense set.

\subsection*{8. Philosophical Point}

This distinction reveals why functional analysis is subtle:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Separability} tells us the space is "small enough" for countable methods
\item \textbf{Schauder basis} tells us the space has "good coordinates" for computations
\item \textbf{Distribution theory} needs to work in spaces that are separable but may lack nice bases
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Summary}

\textbf{No, separable spaces need not have Schauder bases.} This is precisely why:

\begin{itemize}
\item $\mathcal{D}(\Omega)$ can be separable yet lack a basis
\item Distribution theory needs both the heavy TVS machinery AND separability arguments
\item We have different techniques for $\mathcal{S}$ (with basis) vs $\mathcal{D}$ (separable but basisless)
\end{itemize}

Our earlier discussion was preparing you for this exact subtlety: the difference between "topologically small" (separable) and "structurally nice" (with basis) is fundamental to understanding why different distribution spaces require different mathematical tools!
\end{answer} \section{Just to clarify: to have biorthogonal injections one must have a Schauder basis?}

\begin{question}
Just to clarify: to have biorthogonal injections one must have a Schauder basis?
\end{question}

\begin{answer}
\textbf{Excellent clarifying question!} The answer reveals an important nuance:

\subsection*{Short Answer}
\textbf{No, you do NOT need a Schauder basis to have biorthogonal injections.} However, a Schauder basis gives you a particularly \textbf{nice} biorthogonal injection.

Let me clarify the hierarchy:

\subsection*{1. Minimal Requirement: Any Biorthogonal System}

To define an injection $V \to V^*$ via biorthogonality, you only need:
\begin{itemize}
\item A biorthogonal system $\{x_i, f_i\}$ with $x_i \in V$, $f_i \in V^*$
\item Where $f_i(x_j) = \delta_{ij}$
\end{itemize}

Then the map $x_i \mapsto f_i$ (extended linearly) gives an injection.

\textbf{This is much weaker than a Schauder basis!}

\subsection*{2. What a Schauder Basis Adds}

A Schauder basis $\{e_n\}$ gives you a biorthogonal system \textbf{plus}:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Completeness:} $\overline{\text{span}}\{e_n\} = V$
\item \textbf{Continuity:} The coordinate functionals $f_n$ are automatically continuous
\item \textbf{Convergence:} Every vector has a convergent expansion $x = \sum f_n(x)e_n$
\end{enumerate}

So with a Schauder basis, you get a \textbf{continuous injection with dense range}.

\subsection*{3. Weaker Notions That Still Give Injections}

\subsubsection*{Markushevich Basis (M-basis)}
A biorthogonal system $\{x_i, f_i\}$ where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\{f_i\}$ is total (separates points of $V$)
\item $\{x_i\}$ is fundamental (dense span)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{This gives a continuous injection $V \to V^*$} but the basis might not have the nice convergence properties of a Schauder basis.

\subsubsection*{Total Biorthogonal System}
Even weaker: just a biorthogonal system where $\{f_i\}$ separates points.

\textbf{This still gives an injection $V \to V^*$} but the range might not be dense and the map might not be continuous.

\subsection*{4. Concrete Example in Distribution Theory}

\subsubsection*{$\mathcal{D}(\Omega)$ - No Schauder Basis, But...}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{No Schauder basis} (as we discussed)
\item \textbf{But biorthogonal systems exist!} For example, take any countable linearly independent set $\{\phi_n\} \subset \mathcal{D}(\Omega)$
\item By Hahn-Banach, we can find $\{T_n\} \subset \mathcal{D}'(\Omega)$ with $T_i(\phi_j) = \delta_{ij}$
\item This gives an injection $\mathcal{D}(\Omega) \to \mathcal{D}'(\Omega)$
\end{itemize}

The catch: these $T_n$ might be "ugly" and the injection might not be continuous or have nice properties.

\subsection*{5. The Quality Spectrum}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Structure} & \textbf{Injection Quality} \\
\hline
\textbf{Schauder basis} & Continuous injection, dense range, nice convergence \\
\hline
\textbf{M-basis} & Continuous injection, dense range \\
\hline
\textbf{Total biorthogonal system} & Injection (may not be continuous) \\
\hline
\textbf{Any biorthogonal system} & Algebraic injection \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Quality of biorthogonal injections for different structures}
\end{table}

\subsection*{6. Back to Our Distribution Spaces}

\subsubsection*{$\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ - Has Schauder Basis}
\begin{itemize}
\item Hermite basis gives a \textbf{beautiful continuous injection} $\mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{S}'$
\item Well-behaved, computable, everything works nicely
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{$\mathcal{D}(\Omega)$ - No Schauder Basis}
\begin{itemize}
\item Still has biorthogonal systems (by Axiom of Choice)
\item Can define injections $\mathcal{D} \to \mathcal{D}'$
\item But these injections are \textbf{not natural, not continuous, not useful for computations}
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Summary}

\textbf{To have some biorthogonal injection $V \to V^*$:}
\begin{itemize}
\item You only need \textbf{any biorthogonal system}
\item This exists if $\dim(V) \leq \dim(V^*)$ (set-theoretically)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{To have a nice, continuous, computable biorthogonal injection:}
\begin{itemize}
\item A \textbf{Schauder basis} is ideal
\item An \textbf{M-basis} is the next best thing
\item General biorthogonal systems give injections but they might be "useless" in practice
\end{itemize}

This is why in distribution theory, we prefer to work with $\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ when we can - the existence of a Schauder basis gives us well-behaved biorthogonal injections that we can actually compute with!
\end{answer} \section{Is there a name for spaces that have biorthogonal systems?}

\begin{question}
Is there a name for spaces that have biorthogonal systems?
\end{question}

\begin{answer}
\textbf{Yes!} There are several important names and classifications for spaces with biorthogonal systems. The terminology depends on exactly what kind of biorthogonal system you have.

\subsection*{1. The General Case: Spaces with Biorthogonal Systems}

A Banach space $X$ is said to \textbf{admit a biorthogonal system} if there exist $\{x_i\}_{i\in I} \subset X$ and $\{f_i\}_{i\in I} \subset X^*$ with $f_i(x_j) = \delta_{ij}$.

\textbf{This property is actually very common} - most "reasonable" Banach spaces have biorthogonal systems.

\subsection*{2. Important Special Cases with Specific Names}

\subsubsection*{A. Spaces with Markushevich Basis (M-basis)}
This is probably the most important and well-studied case.

An \textbf{M-basis} is a biorthogonal system $\{x_i, f_i\}$ where:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\{x_i\}$ is \textbf{fundamental}: $\overline{\text{span}}\{x_i\} = X$
\item $\{f_i\}$ is \textbf{total}: if $f_i(x) = 0$ for all $i$, then $x = 0$
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Properties:}
\begin{itemize}
\item Gives a continuous injection $X \to X^*$
\item Every separable Banach space has an M-basis! (Markushevich, 1943)
\item But the biorthogonal functionals might not be uniformly bounded
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{B. Spaces with Schauder Basis}
As we've discussed - this is the "gold standard" where everything works nicely.

\subsubsection*{C. Spaces with Unconditional Basis}
Even stronger - the basis representations converge unconditionally.

\subsubsection*{D. Spaces with Bounded M-basis}
An M-basis where $\sup_i \|x_i\|\|f_i\| < \infty$.
This ensures the associated projection operators are uniformly bounded.

\subsection*{3. Hierarchy of Properties}

Here's how these concepts relate:

\[
\text{Schauder Basis} \Rightarrow \text{Unconditional Basis (sometimes)}
\]
\[
\Rightarrow \text{Bounded M-basis} \Rightarrow \text{Markushevich Basis} \Rightarrow \text{Existence of Biorthogonal System}
\]

\textbf{Key facts:}
\begin{itemize}
\item Every separable Banach space has an M-basis
\item But not every separable Banach space has a Schauder basis (Enflo)
\item The step from M-basis to Schauder basis is huge
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{4. Special Names for Dual Relationships}

\subsubsection*{A. Quasi-reflexive Spaces}
Spaces where $\dim(X^{**}/X) < \infty$. These often have very nice biorthogonal structure.

\subsubsection*{B. Spaces with the Density Property}
$X$ has the density property if there exists an injection $X \to X^*$ with dense range. This is equivalent to having a total biorthogonal system.

\subsubsection*{C. Locally Convex Spaces with Equicontinuous Basis}
In the broader context of locally convex spaces, this is the generalization of Schauder bases.

\subsection*{5. Negative Results and Limitations}

\subsubsection*{A. The Johnson-Lindenstrauss Spaces}
There exist Banach spaces (JL spaces) that are \textbf{hereditarily indecomposable} - they have no nontrivial biorthogonal systems with special properties.

\subsubsection*{B. Spaces Without M-bases}
While every separable Banach space has an M-basis, there exist \textbf{non-separable} Banach spaces without M-bases.

\subsection*{6. Applications to Specific Spaces}

\subsubsection*{Classical Sequence Spaces}
\begin{itemize}
\item $\ell^p$ ($1 \leq p < \infty$): Have unconditional bases
\item $c_0$: Has Schauder basis
\item $\ell^\infty$: Has no Schauder basis (not separable) but has biorthogonal systems
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Function Spaces}  
\begin{itemize}
\item $L^p[0,1]$ ($1 < p < \infty$): Have unconditional bases
\item $C[0,1]$: Has Schauder basis (Faber-Schauder)
\item $L^1[0,1]$: Has no unconditional basis
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{7. Modern Research Directions}

Current research focuses on:

\subsubsection*{A. Biorthogonal Systems in Non-separable Spaces}
Understanding which non-separable spaces have "nice" biorthogonal systems.

\subsubsection*{B. Applications to Operator Theory}
Using biorthogonal systems to study the structure of operators.

\subsubsection*{C. Connections to Set Theory}
Some questions about existence of biorthogonal systems are independent of ZFC.

\subsection*{Summary}

\textbf{The main terminology is:}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{"Spaces with biorthogonal systems"} (most general)
\item \textbf{"Spaces with Markushevich bases"} (very common for separable spaces)  
\item \textbf{"Spaces with Schauder bases"} (strong and useful)
\item \textbf{"Spaces with unconditional bases"} (even stronger)
\end{itemize}

For a separable Banach space, having an M-basis is essentially the \textbf{weakest reasonable biorthogonal structure} - it's enough to get a continuous injection into the dual, but doesn't guarantee the nice computational properties of a Schauder basis.

This hierarchy explains why distribution theory can work with both $\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ (beautiful Schauder basis) and $\mathcal{D}(\Omega)$ (only general biorthogonal systems) - both have enough structure to define distributions as dual elements, but the quality of that structure differs dramatically!
\end{answer} \section{What about spaces that are not Banach?}

\begin{question}
What about spaces that are not Banach?
\end{question}

\begin{answer}
\textbf{Excellent question!} The theory of biorthogonal systems becomes much richer and more nuanced for spaces that are not Banach. The completeness and norm structure of Banach spaces provide crucial simplifications that disappear in more general settings.

\subsection*{1. The Locally Convex Case}

\subsubsection*{Nuclear Spaces (like $\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)$, $\mathcal{D}(\Omega)$)}
These are where distribution theory lives:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{$\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)$}: Has Schauder basis (Hermite functions)
\item \textbf{$\mathcal{D}(\Omega)$}: No Schauder basis, but has biorthogonal systems
\item \textbf{Key point}: The biorthogonal functionals are automatically continuous due to nuclearity
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Fréchet Spaces}
\begin{itemize}
\item May or may not have bases
\item The biorthogonal functionals (if they exist) are continuous (by open mapping theorem)
\item Many classical Fréchet spaces of analytic functions have bases
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{LF-spaces (like $\mathcal{D}(\Omega)$)}
\begin{itemize}
\item Strict inductive limits of Fréchet spaces
\item Biorthogonal systems exist but are complicated
\item The topology is too weak to guarantee nice basis properties
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{2. The Non-Locally Convex Case}

\subsubsection*{F-spaces (Complete metric TVS, not locally convex)}
\begin{itemize}
\item Biorthogonal systems can exist but behave pathologically
\item Coordinate functionals might be discontinuous
\item The Hahn-Banach theorem fails, so extending biorthogonal systems is problematic
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Example: $L^p[0,1]$ for $0 < p < 1$}
\begin{itemize}
\item Not locally convex
\item Has no continuous linear functionals except $0$!
\item \textbf{Cannot have any biorthogonal system} with continuous functionals
\item The dual space is trivial: $\{0\}$
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{3. The Non-Metrizable Case}

\subsubsection*{General Locally Convex Spaces}
\begin{itemize}
\item Biorthogonal systems can exist
\item But convergence issues become subtle
\item May need to work with weak topologies
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Example: $\mathbb{R}^\infty$ (countable product of $\mathbb{R}$)}
\begin{itemize}
\item Locally convex, complete, but not metrizable
\item Has Schauder-like bases
\item But convergence is coordinate-wise, not uniform
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{4. The Non-Complete Case}

\subsubsection*{Normed Spaces that aren't Banach}
\begin{itemize}
\item Biorthogonal systems can exist
\item But the biorthogonal functionals might not extend to the completion
\item The associated "biorthogonal injection" might not have closed range
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Example: Polynomials in $C[0,1]$}
\begin{itemize}
\item Dense subspace of $C[0,1]$
\item Has algebraic bases (monomials)
\item But the coordinate functionals are unbounded
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{5. Special Phenomena in Non-Banach Settings}

\subsubsection*{Discontinuous Biorthogonal Systems}
In incomplete spaces, you can have biorthogonal systems where the coordinate functionals are discontinuous.

\subsubsection*{Algebraic vs Topological Bases}
The distinction becomes crucial:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Algebraic basis}: Hamel basis, works for any vector space
\item \textbf{Topological basis}: Requires convergence in the topology
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{The Role of Completeness}
Completeness ensures that:
\begin{itemize}
\item Continuous linear functionals extend
\item Uniform boundedness principles apply
\item Many "pathological" examples are excluded
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{6. Positive Results for Nice Non-Banach Spaces}

\subsubsection*{Nuclear Fréchet Spaces}
Often have excellent biorthogonal structure:
\begin{itemize}
\item Bases exist for many classical spaces
\item Coordinate functionals are continuous
\item Kernel theorems work beautifully
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Schwartz Spaces}
The theory of distributions relies on the fact that $\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ and $\mathcal{D}(\Omega)$, while not Banach, are \textbf{complete, Hausdorff, locally convex} and have enough structure to support a rich duality theory.

\subsection*{7. Negative Results and Pathologies}

\subsubsection*{Spaces with Trivial Dual}
As mentioned: $L^p[0,1]$ for $0 < p < 1$ has no nonzero continuous linear functionals, so no biorthogonal systems.

\subsubsection*{Spaces Where Hahn-Banach Fails}
Without Hahn-Banach, you cannot extend biorthogonal systems from subspaces.

\subsubsection*{Non-Separable Non-Metrizable Spaces}
Can have bizarre biorthogonal structure that depends on set-theoretic axioms.

\subsection*{8. The Big Picture: What Survives and What Doesn't}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Property} & \textbf{Banach Spaces} & \textbf{Nice LCS} & \textbf{General TVS} \\
\hline
\textbf{Hahn-Banach} & Yes & Yes & No \\
\hline
\textbf{Uniform Boundedness} & Yes & For Fréchet & No \\
\hline
\textbf{Automatic Continuity} & Often & For Fréchet & Rarely \\
\hline
\textbf{Biorthogonal Systems} & Common & Common & Pathological \\
\hline
\textbf{Useful Bases} & Schauder bases & Sometimes & Rarely \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Properties in different types of topological vector spaces}
\end{table}

\subsection*{9. Why Distribution Theory Works}

The spaces in distribution theory are carefully chosen to be \textbf{just nice enough}:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{$\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)$}: Nuclear Fréchet space - excellent basis theory
\item \textbf{$\mathcal{D}(\Omega)$}: LF-space - complicated but separable with continuous dual
\item Both are \textbf{complete, Hausdorff, locally convex}
\item Both have \textbf{rich dual spaces} (distributions)
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Summary}

\textbf{For non-Banach spaces, biorthogonal systems:}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Can exist} in nice locally convex spaces (Fréchet, nuclear spaces)
\item \textbf{Behave pathologically} in non-locally convex spaces
\item \textbf{Might not exist at all} in spaces with trivial dual
\item \textbf{Require careful analysis} of continuity and convergence
\end{itemize}

The theory becomes a delicate balance: you need enough structure to do meaningful analysis, but not so much that you exclude important spaces like $\mathcal{D}(\Omega)$. This is why distribution theory represents such a beautiful compromise in functional analysis - it finds the "sweet spot" where generalized functions can live and where a powerful duality theory can be developed, even without the full strength of Banach space theory!
\end{answer} 